85	Jan Vishwas Act 2023 - Chapter II: Amendments to Information Technology Act 2000	Section 67C	Failure of Intermediary to Retain Information	Requires intermediaries to preserve and retain specified information for a period determined by the Central Government; failure to do so invites penalty.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine, or both.	\N
86	Jan Vishwas Act 2023 - Chapter II: Amendments to Information Technology Act 2000	Section 72A	Disclosure of Personal Information Without Consent	Penalises service providers or intermediaries who disclose personal information obtained under a lawful contract without consent or in breach of confidentiality.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to â‚¹5 lakh, or both.	\N
87	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter IX: Penalties and Adjudication	Section 43	Penalty for damage to computer, computer system, etc.	Unauthorized access, introducing virus, deleting or altering data, or causing denial of service to legitimate users.	Liable to pay compensation to the affected person.	\N
88	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter IX: Penalties and Adjudication	Section 43A	Compensation for failure to protect data	Companies handling sensitive personal data must ensure reasonable security practices; failure makes them liable for damages.	Compensation to affected individuals.	\N
89	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 65	Tampering with computer source documents	Intentionally concealing, destroying, or altering computer source code required to be kept by law.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to â‚¹2 lakh, or both.	\N
90	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66	Computer-related offences	Covers hacking, unauthorized system access, or causing wrongful loss through digital means.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to â‚¹5 lakh, or both.	\N
91	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66B	Receiving stolen computer resources	Buying, receiving, or using stolen computer resources or communication devices knowingly.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
92	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66C	Identity theft	Fraudulently using another person's password, digital signature, or unique identification.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, and fine up to â‚¹1 lakh.	\N
93	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66D	Cheating by personation using computer resources	Online scams, impersonation, phishing, or fake job offers through digital platforms.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, and fine up to â‚¹1 lakh.	\N
94	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66E	Violation of privacy	Capturing, publishing, or transmitting images of a person's private area without consent.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to â‚¹2 lakh, or both.	\N
95	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 66F	Cyber terrorism	Acts using computers or networks that threaten the unity, integrity, or security of India.	Imprisonment for life.	\N
96	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 67	Publishing obscene material in electronic form	Publishing or transmitting obscene or sexually explicit material online.	First conviction: 3 years imprisonment and â‚¹5 lakh fine; subsequent conviction: 5 years imprisonment and â‚¹10 lakh fine.	\N
97	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 67A	Publishing sexually explicit material	Publishing or transmitting sexually explicit content in electronic form.	Imprisonment up to 5 years and fine up to â‚¹10 lakh.	\N
98	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 67B	Child pornography	Depicting children in sexually explicit acts, or facilitating such acts online.	Imprisonment up to 5 years and fine up to â‚¹10 lakh for first conviction.	\N
99	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 68	Failure to obey directions of Controller	Non-compliance with lawful directions issued by the Controller under the Act.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
100	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 69	Power to intercept, monitor or decrypt information	Government may authorize interception or monitoring for public order and national security.	Failure to comply may result in imprisonment up to 7 years.	\N
101	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 69A	Power to block websites and information	Government can block access to online content for national security or public order.	Non-compliance can result in imprisonment up to 7 years and fine.	\N
102	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 69B	Monitoring and collection of traffic data	Allows monitoring of internet traffic for cybersecurity or law enforcement purposes.	Non-compliance or misuse may attract penalties under the Act.	\N
103	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 70	Protected systems	Accessing or attempting to access protected government systems without authorization.	Imprisonment up to 10 years and fine.	\N
104	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 70A	National Critical Information Infrastructure Protection Centre (NCIIPC)	Establishes authority to protect national critical information infrastructure.	Penalties as prescribed for violation or negligence.	\N
105	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 70B	Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In)	Handles cybersecurity incidents, issues alerts, and ensures compliance.	Failure to report or comply may attract penalties.	\N
106	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 71	Penalty for misrepresentation	Providing false information to obtain a digital signature certificate.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
107	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 72	Breach of confidentiality and privacy	Unauthorized access or sharing of personal information obtained lawfully.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
108	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 73	Publishing false digital signature certificates	Issuing or publishing false or fraudulent electronic signature certificates.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
109	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XI: Offences	Section 74	Publication for fraudulent purpose	Using electronic certificates or signatures for fraudulent activities.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to â‚¹1 lakh, or both.	\N
110	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XII: Miscellaneous	Section 75	Extra-territorial jurisdiction	Act applies to offences committed outside India involving Indian computer systems or citizens.	As per provisions of the Act.	\N
111	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XII: Miscellaneous	Section 76	Confiscation	Seizure of any computer, device, or data involved in an offence.	Equipment and data may be confiscated by authorities.	\N
112	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XII: Miscellaneous	Section 77	Compensation and penalties	Defines general compensation and penalties under the Act.	As prescribed depending on the offence.	\N
113	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XII: Miscellaneous	Section 77A	Compounding of offences	Allows certain offences to be settled by mutual agreement before trial.	Subject to court approval.	\N
114	Information Technology Act, 2000 - Chapter XII: Miscellaneous	Section 77B	Bailable and cognizable offences	Defines which offences under the IT Act are bailable or cognizable.	Procedural provision; defines classification.	\N
115	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XVI: Offences Affecting the Human Body	Section 354A/354C/354D	Sexual harassment, voyeurism, and stalking	Online stalking, sending obscene messages, or capturing private images without consent.	Imprisonment up to 3 years and fine.	\N
116	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XVII: Offences Against Property	Section 379	Theft	Theft of data, devices, or digital property.	Imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine, or both.	\N
117	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XVII: Offences Against Property	Section 406/409	Criminal breach of trust	Unauthorized misuse of data or funds entrusted to a person.	Imprisonment up to 10 years and fine.	\N
118	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XVII: Offences Against Property	Section 417/420	Cheating and dishonesty	Online scams, fake websites, or impersonation for monetary gain.	Imprisonment up to 7 years and fine.	\N
119	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XVIII: Offences Relating to Documents and Property Marks	Section 468/469/471	Forgery and use of forged documents	Creating or using fake online identities or forged electronic records.	Imprisonment up to 7 years and fine.	\N
120	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XXI: Defamation	Section 499/500	Defamation	Spreading false or defamatory content online to harm reputation.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine, or both.	\N
121	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XXII: Criminal Intimidation, Insult, and Annoyance	Section 503/506	Criminal intimidation and threats	Threats, blackmail, or harassment through emails or social media.	Imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine, or both.	\N
122	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XXII: Criminal Intimidation, Insult, and Annoyance	Section 509	Insulting modesty of a woman	Sending obscene or indecent messages to women online.	Imprisonment up to 3 years and fine.	\N
123	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XXIII: Attempts, Abetments, and Conspiracies	Section 120B	Criminal conspiracy	Planning or coordinating cyberattacks or group online frauds.	Same as the main offence planned.	\N
124	Indian Penal Code - Chapter XIV: Offences Affecting the Public Health, Safety, Convenience, Decency, and Morals	Section 292/294	Obscene material and acts	Publishing or transmitting obscene content on digital platforms.	Imprisonment up to 2 years and fine.	\N
125	Indian Evidence Act, 1872 - Chapter V: Documentary Evidence	Section 65A	Special provisions for electronic records	Specifies conditions for using electronic documents as evidence.	Procedural provision; no direct punishment.	\N
126	Indian Evidence Act, 1872 - Chapter V: Documentary Evidence	Section 65B	Admissibility of electronic evidence	Digital evidence like emails or chats must be certified to be admissible in court.	Procedural provision; no direct punishment.	\N
